he Russian  Federation at nearly five times the OECD  average this problem is compounded by  the existence of private for-profit  prisons and the underreporting of  incarceration rates in some states in  terms of childhood health and economic  status America performs very poorly  compared to its developed peers as of  2013 the US infant mortality rate was  the highest in the developed world for  the children that survived the outlook  is still pretty bleak the youth poverty  rate in the United States is the high  across the OECD with fully one-quarter  of youth living in poverty the Stanford  center on poverty and inequality says  the u.s. is a clear and consistent  outlier in the child poverty league as  US child poverty rates are continually  the highest among the six richest  countries the Stanford center also ranks  the most well-off countries in terms of  labor markets poverty safety net wealth  inequality and economic mobility the US  comes in dead last of the top ten most  well-off countries and 18th among the  top 21 the report makes it clear that  the u.s. holds some pretty undesirable  titles but why is that how can such a  wealthy powerful and technologically  advanced nation have such extreme  economic disparity part of the problem  stems from America's stance on economic  and social rights successive  administrations have repeatedly rejected  the idea that these economic and social  rights are full-fledged human rights  despite their clear recognition in  treaties the US has ratified  international human rights law  recognizes the right to education a  right to health care a right to social  protection for those in need and a right  to an adequate standard of living the  report states that in practice the  United States is alone among developed  countries in insisting that while human  rights are of fundamental importance  they do not include rights that guard  against dying of hunger dying from a  lack of access to affordable health care  or growing up in a context of total  deprivation if the US doesn't  acknowledge these needs as basic human  rights then it's no surprise there are  so many people living in extreme poverty  the nation's wealth is funneled into  things deemed more critical most notably  the bloated national defense budget so  if the people running the country don't  want to fix these problems what can we  do that's where the second part of the  problem comes in the United States is  supposed to be a nation by the people  and for the people but for some reason  or several reasons the people aren't  speaking up only about 55 and a half  percent of the u.s. voting age  population cast ballots in the 2016  presidential election in the OECD the  u.s. came 28th and voter turnout  compared with an average of 75 percent  registered voters also represent a much  smaller share of potential voters in the  u.s. than just about any other OECD  country only about 64% of the u.s.  voting age population was registered in  2016 compared with 91% in Canada and the  UK 96% in Sweden and near  9% in Japan these two issues form a  vicious cycle the administration doesn't  acknowledge the growing problem with  extreme wealth inequality in the country  the people don't vote in leaders who do  want to fix the problem and it happens  all over again disturbingly it's not  just the fault of unmotivated voters in  just this past election we've seen  dramatic gerrymandering of electoral  districts to privilege particular groups  of voters the imposition of artificial  and unnecessary voter ID requirements  the blatant manipulation of polling  station locations and the relocating of  DMVs to make it more difficult for  certain groups to obtain IDs this has  resulted in people living in poverty  minorities and other disfavored groups  being systematically deprived of their  voting rights and so the cycle continues  the poor stay poor because they're not  represented and often can't even vote to  elect someone who might make a  difference as more and more children are  born into poverty and the economic  divide grows we'll begin to see a very  paradoxical crisis in which one of the  wealthiest nations on earth hosts the  largest population of homeless starving  uneducated and deeply impoverished ok so  the US has some problems but what can we  do about it  what can the average citizen do to  change a political and societal  atmosphere that completely ostracizes  such a large portion of the population  that's the million-dollar question or  rather several billion dollar question  as large scale changes to social  policies will likely incur a huge cost  but at the end of the day most people  would hopefully say it's worth it as  more and more people realize their  government might not have their best  interest at heart it wouldn't be such a  surprise to see the next few generations  of voters speaking up and demanding real  changes to the way the country is run is  comparing very different nations the  best way to measure success or failure  maybe maybe not but highlighting ways in  which any country has room to improve  should be met with gratitude since  improving our lives and those of our  neighbors is all that really matters in  the end what do you think is the United  States on the right track or has it gone  off the rails in recent years what  changes would you like to see to  economic or social policy let us know  your thoughts in the comments below here  on second thought I'd like to start the  new year with a new resolution this year  my resolution is to learn more advanced  animation techniques from my videos to  do that I'm using classes from  Skillshare Skillshare is an online  learning community  for creators featuring over 17,000  classes in graphic design animation  photography and more that could help you  learn a new skill a premium membership  from Skillshare is more affordable than  most other online learning platforms and  gives you unlimited access to amazing  classes 